pub const INSTRUCTIONS: &str = "Task backlog server for a single user. Use tasks_*, projects_*, scopes_* and tags_list tools to list, create, update and delete tasks, projects and scopes. tags_list returns the tag dictionary. tasks_revisions_list reads the previous description and comment texts of a task. tasks_deleted_list returns the soft-deleted tasks, the ones that tasks_restore can bring back. attachments_list, attachments_add, attachments_delete and attachments_verify work with the files of a task: they are kept in the media folder, and attachments_verify reports the files that are gone from it. attachments_read returns the file itself, an image as an image block. Every attachment is also a resource: read xboct-log://attachment/{id} with resources/read to get its bytes, take the uri from attachments_list. The resource xboct-log://attachments lists them all. Descriptions and comments are Markdown and formatting is rendered: format them, and put a short log, config or command output in a fenced code block. Keep source code in the repository and describe the algorithm instead, and stay short - no walls of text. A raw log or dump that is really needed belongs in an attachment, not pasted into the text. backup_create makes a manual database backup, backup_list shows the existing snapshots. Restoring a backup is not part of these tools: the user restores a snapshot on the Backups page of the UI, tell them which one. Check isError before reading the payload: a failed call explains the cause in the text, and errors raised by the tool itself also carry structuredContent.error = { kind, message }. Create tasks in batches with tasks_create_many: it answers with one result per item instead of one call per task. All tools accept a single JSON object argument. Task tools address a task by task_id in the full 'PREFIX-NUMBER' form (e.g. 'LOG-42'); attachments address a file by attachment_id, the `id` field of an item from attachments_list. Plans are global and have no project_id: read one plan's ordered items with tasks_list {plan_id, sort: 'plan_position', include_deleted: true}, its notes with plans_notes_list and its document links with plans_documents_list. Documents are project-owned and addressed by their internal id or 'PREFIX-NNN' key; documents_list and plans_documents_list return short_body by default, set include_body to read the full text, and accept project_id/kind/status/q/title/title_exact/source_file filters, sort/order, fields, include_deleted/deleted_only, limit/offset and total. Set include_plan_names on tasks_list to get readable plan references; tasks_get always returns plan_names. Every task returned by create, update, close, move, link and batch operations carries the same plan_names together with the unchanged plan_ids UUID list.";

pub const ATTACHMENTS_READ_DESCRIPTION: &str = "Read the file of one attachment, not only its metadata. An image comes back as an image block, text as text, anything else as base64 in a data URL. Use it to look at a screenshot attached to a task instead of searching the disk for the file. An attachment is at most 20 MB and a big one costs a lot of context, so check size in attachments_list first. A file too big to read can be downloaded from /media/{id} of this server instead.";

pub const RESOURCES_DESCRIPTION: &str = "Every attachment of the backlog with the uri that holds its bytes. Read it when attachments may have been added or removed since the client connected.";

pub const RESOURCES_TITLE: &str = "Task attachments";

pub const TEMPLATE_TITLE: &str = "Task attachment";

pub const TEMPLATE_DESCRIPTION: &str = "The file of one task attachment, base64-encoded. Take the uri from attachments_list. An attachment is at most 20 MB.";

